00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:39.820
And we've been working
with Home Depot

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.910
to get their website,
HomeDepot.com,

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.090
which is a big e-commerce
website, to get

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.400
it running on Google Cloud.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.380
And I wanted to
give them a chance

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.930
to tell their story about
what their experience is

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:56.230
of building stuff
on Google Cloud

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.250
and how they do
metrics, how they

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:06.280
do multiple zonal resiliency
and stuff, and their experience

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.930
with CRE and
building an SRE team.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.380
So I'm going to hand
it over to William,

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.320
who is the director
of SRE for Home Depot

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.120
and is responsible for keeping
Home Depot Online online.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.129
William?

00:01:20.129 --> 00:01:22.420
WILLIAM: Well, and the only
thing I'm going to say here

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.782
is that Luke and I just
did a previous session.

00:01:24.782 --> 00:01:26.740
So I don't know if anybody
was in that session,

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.448
and I promised a few
people that we would

00:01:28.448 --> 00:01:29.920
answer more detailed questions.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.037
So I wasn't the really
deep technology guy

00:01:33.037 --> 00:01:34.120
to answer those questions.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.390
But we got Jay,
Cuong, and Kumar,

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.970
who are SREs at Home Depot.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:41.470
And if you guys
have some questions,

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:43.150
they're going to talk
about a few things

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:44.399
that you may find of interest.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:47.211
And if you guys have any
questions, feel free to ask,

00:01:47.211 --> 00:01:47.710
all right?

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.558
Without further
ado, who's first?

00:01:50.558 --> 00:01:56.402
[INAUDIBLE]

00:01:56.402 --> 00:01:59.324
LUKE STONE: OK,
here's our agenda.

00:01:59.324 --> 00:02:02.130
Our agenda, real
quick, is Cuong is

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.400
going to talk about
VALET, which is basically

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:11.970
Home Depot's version of
SLOs, SLAs, you know?

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.270
And Kumar's going to talk
about zonal resiliency

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:20.280
and their interconnection with
their on-premise environment.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.160
And Jay's going to
talk about CRE/SRE.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.310
Take it away.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.370
CUONG: Hello, everybody.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:28.710
My name is Cuong.

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.650
I've been working with the
Google Cloud Platform project

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.370
at Home Depot for
the past two years.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.430
We are not only leveraging
the Google Cloud Platform

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.400
and putting microservices
on the Cloud,

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.340
but actually we have been
transforming our web engineer

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:44.920
into an SRE.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.820
Well, the best way to learn
is from the best, Google SRE.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.750
So we have adopted many of
the principle and concept

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.660
from Google SRE.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.605
One of them is the SLO.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.140
We require often
microservices before going

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.570
live to have a well-defined SLO.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:16.440
And the responsibility
is between the SRE

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.940
and the development team, and
that could mean reaching out

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.460
to other stakeholder.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.550
LUKE STONE: So how many
microservices do you have?

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:35.750
CUONG: We probably
have about 70, yes.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:38.800
LUKE STONE: So you
have to kind of keep

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.670
the SLO somewhat
standardized across all

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.290
of these microservices.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:43.880
CUONG: Yes, we do.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:48.500
We have five key
metrics to our SLO.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.440
We call it VALET,
which is Volume,

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.550
Availability,
Latency, and Error.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.820
And then the last
one is tickets.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:08.230
And most of these, we get
it from our logging system.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:15.130
And we're using SLOs for
tracking and monitoring.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:21.709
Also, every week we're
going through all of the SLO

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:27.460
or incident to see if any of the
service is breaching the SLO.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.067
LUKE STONE: So what happens
if a service goes out of SLO?

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:33.400
CUONG: We're going to have
an SRE and a development team

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:38.080
to dig down and find
out what's the problem

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.770
and resolve the issue.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.450
And many of the
new microservice,

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.085
before it's going
live, we require

00:04:45.085 --> 00:04:49.330
it going through a slew
of tests, of load tests,

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:50.780
and destructive tests.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.500
And basically, what
it does is helping

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:57.730
the SRE and the development
team to understand the service

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:58.390
better.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:02.500
And based on that, either
they adjust the SLO

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:08.200
before it's going live, or
making changes on the service,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.820
to make sure that it's
below the SLO requirement.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:13.370
LUKE STONE: Yeah, so at
Google, the tradition

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:17.130
is if you miss your SLO, then--

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.990
if you go out of SLO in a
month, then you basically

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:23.890
stop pushing new features.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:27.730
And you shift your work
over towards reliability.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:30.451
Everybody has a backlog
of reliability stuff

00:05:30.451 --> 00:05:31.450
that they can chew into.

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.530
Do you guys do
anything like that?

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.250
CUONG: Yeah, like I
say, we are transforming

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:38.950
our culture and SRE.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:42.370
So yeah, we start
enforcing some of those.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.260
Again, but I mean,
that is the culture

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.845
that we are moving forward here.

00:05:49.845 --> 00:05:51.220
LUKE STONE: And
so do you require

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:55.850
people to create VALET
metrics before they

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:57.250
launch their service?

00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:00.100
CUONG: Yes, like I said,
before any microservice,

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:02.500
before it's launched
live, we require

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.650
it to have a well-defined SLO.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.220
And then, like I
said, before it's

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:09.820
going live, we go through a
load test and destructive test

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:13.440
and make sure it met the SLO.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.230
Or potentially, that's
just when the time where

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:20.860
they adjust the SLO to make sure
that it's met the requirement.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.726
LUKE STONE: Who's the decider on
what's acceptable for the SLOs?

00:06:24.726 --> 00:06:27.760
CUONG: Well, we
have a stakeholder

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:29.200
mostly on our development team.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.080
And that's why I say, SRE
and the development team

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.712
work together to agree
on a certain SLO.

00:06:36.712 --> 00:06:37.420
LUKE STONE: Nice.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:42.250
So do you roll
these SLOs all up,

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.020
because there's also volume
in there, which isn't really

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:45.820
an SLO thing.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:49.246
But do you roll them all up
to make a composite score?

00:06:49.246 --> 00:06:50.370
CUONG: Currently, we don't.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.540
We look at them as a separate.

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:56.700
So any of those breaking,
then that is breaking SLO.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:00.600
So right now, we don't have
a one composite number.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:06.930
LUKE STONE: So in Google SRE,
we have an idea on tickets.

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:09.720
I guess we have more
of a rule of thumb

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:13.920
that you shouldn't get paged
more than twice in a shift.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:16.380
Is that somewhat what you
use these tickets for?

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:17.970
CUONG: Yeah, right
now, the default we

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.730
have, one or two ticket
on most of the SLO.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:25.800
But I don't think
we are strictly

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:26.850
applying that right now.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.630
But it's something we
probably-- like I say,

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:31.080
it's a culture
that we're probably

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.828
going to start
enforcing more as we go.

00:07:34.828 --> 00:07:35.536
LUKE STONE: Nice.

00:07:35.536 --> 00:07:37.490
And so how do you--

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:39.340
when you have a service
that has latency

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:42.630
because it depends on
another service behind it,

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:44.880
how do you figure out what's
an appropriate latency,

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.690
or how do you
measure that latency?

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:49.880
CUONG: Yeah, I think
all the dependency--

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:54.777
so the latency is dependent
on, also, aggregate

00:07:54.777 --> 00:07:55.860
with all the dependencies.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.940
So if your microservice
latency is 100,

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.970
and then if it's
beyond, above, that,

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:08.270
and that including all the
dependencies-- so we don't--

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.290
we just take the aggregate
as a whole, yeah.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:11.581
LUKE STONE: It's just baked in.

00:08:11.581 --> 00:08:13.830
It's just baked in
the whole thing.

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.390
JAY: Some of it's handled
by circuit breaker patterns,

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:19.280
and even where
we've had to adjust

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:22.950
SLOs for lower-level
dependencies

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.786
because we found that,
hey, you know what?

00:08:25.786 --> 00:08:31.350
When we've got a customer
facing API or a service

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:33.570
that has a dependency,
and it's got

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.525
to call this service--
so we can't achieve

00:08:35.525 --> 00:08:36.900
our availability
for this service

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:39.341
if your SLOs are this loose.

00:08:39.341 --> 00:08:41.549
So we've had to tighten some
of those up to make sure

00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:44.910
that we could still hit
our overall availability,

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:47.525
especially for
customer-facing components.

00:08:50.055 --> 00:08:51.430
LUKE STONE: So I
have a question.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:55.890
This is probably
somewhat sensitive.

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.600
But do you have
any services that

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.240
are chronically out of SLO?

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:04.160
CUONG: Actually,
we used to have it,

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.650
and then we actually
changed the SLO.

00:09:09.384 --> 00:09:11.300
LUKE STONE: That's a
good technique sometimes.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.820
Just make the SLO
reflect reality.

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:16.095
Get back into SLO, and
then start incrementally

00:09:16.095 --> 00:09:16.830
improving it.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:19.640
CUONG: But we did talk back
to the other stakeholder,

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:21.530
and they all agreed.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:23.480
Like I say, sometimes
the dependency--

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:26.720
it's taken much longer
than we anticipate,

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:29.260
and that's why
we're changing it.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.580
And I think PIP is
one of the products

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:36.230
that we are about to
roll out in a few weeks.

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:38.270
And I think-- before
it's launching,

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:40.840
and it's constantly
go beyond our SLO.

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:45.410
So that's why the SRE team and
development team constantly

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:48.800
try to make the improvements
before it actually goes live.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:53.360
So before it goes live, we
did a lab test and load test

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.942
to make sure that
the SLO is met.

00:09:56.942 --> 00:09:57.650
LUKE STONE: Nice.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:00.440
I think it's pretty impressive
to see their whole system,

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:03.785
because they basically have a
dashboard that they can plug

00:10:03.785 --> 00:10:06.510
in all of these
SLO metrics into,

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:08.540
that then their business
stakeholders can just

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:10.070
use this dashboard.

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:14.240
And they don't have to
do what some people have

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.150
to do at Google, which is
then go look at the SLO

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:19.592
and try to figure out what it
means, and how it's defined,

00:10:19.592 --> 00:10:21.050
and dig, and dig,
and dig, and dig.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:24.420
It's all really very
clear at Home Depot.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:26.210
It's pretty cool.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:29.450
CUONG: I forgot-- so these
are like some of the items

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:33.080
that typically the SRE
and development team have

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:34.550
to go through and come up with.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:36.480
So typically, we
have a stakeholder.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.960
And then the due
date, typically right

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:42.350
before the launch date-- so
we have to have all these

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:46.960
defined before the
microservice can launch.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:47.980
So let me just go here.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:49.550
LUKE STONE: And the
microservices are pretty--

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:50.799
they're pretty similar, right?

00:10:50.799 --> 00:10:54.855
They're all web-based,
REST kind of API services.

00:10:54.855 --> 00:10:56.330
CUONG: Right, yes,
right-- so here

00:10:56.330 --> 00:11:01.830
is one of the sample
microservice that I have here.

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:07.250
So every week that we are
going through the service,

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:09.980
and make sure that
all have met the SRO.

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:13.520
And in this particular
service, if you look at that,

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:16.010
that is sort of like a volume.

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.020
When we start out
with the service,

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:24.350
we assume that there should
only be around 450 requests.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:27.284
But obviously, that is way off.

00:11:27.284 --> 00:11:28.700
But that actually
is a good thing,

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:31.740
because actually we can handle
much more than we anticipated.

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:38.060
JAY: So this is
actually an application

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:40.730
that we built to help
us track our SLOs.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:45.440
So we're using Stackdriver
for most of our monitoring,

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:50.760
but it's not great to use for
tracking your SLOs over time.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:53.960
So we built the system,
and it takes inputs

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:55.940
from a variety of--

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:59.570
some, ultimately, from
our logs in BigQuery.

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:02.210
We extract data out
of those, and then

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:04.850
push it into-- gather
your aggregated metric

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:09.660
and push it into
this application.

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:12.470
But that also allows us
to expand this beyond just

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:13.790
our Google application.

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:17.700
So the inputs can be
generated from wherever.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:21.350
So we can even use this
for our critical systems

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:23.960
that are in our on-premise
environment as well.

00:12:29.022 --> 00:12:29.730
LUKE STONE: Cool.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:36.370
Is that basically--
any last thoughts?

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:39.470
CUONG: No, basically,
like I said,

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:41.360
we are trying to
change the culture.

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.870
We're not just leveraging
the Google Cloud platform.

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:49.920
We are changing the whole
engineer to become more SRE.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:53.780
And some of these
are new for us,

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:57.780
so we're continuously going
to keep improving and just--

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.230
LUKE STONE: Yeah, it's cool
that you're using data-driven

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:03.350
approach, and it's not just--

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:04.650
you're not winging it.

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:08.380
You have some real, good hard
numbers behind these decisions.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:10.760
All right, thanks, Cuong.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:13.670
So, Kumar, you want to tell us--

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:15.987
what's on the next slide?

00:13:15.987 --> 00:13:17.570
You were going to
tell us a little bit

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:22.340
about HomeDepot.com global
architecture, right?

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:23.780
KUMAR: Yeah, so I'm Kumar.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:25.490
I'm the principal
engineer at Home Depot,

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:28.790
and I work closely
with the SRE team.

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:34.070
So this is the high-level,
10,000-foot architecture

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:38.810
for application hosted on GCP.

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:43.640
So there are other components,
but for the sake of simplicity,

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:44.630
we have omitted it.

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:50.070
And this is like a basic stack
of what an application deployed

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.050
on the Google Cloud looks like.

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:54.440
So if you look at this
on the extreme left,

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:57.350
there is an on-prem service,
which kind of tells you

00:13:57.350 --> 00:13:59.720
that we are in a hybrid model.

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.690
There are services which
are hosted on Home Depot

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:07.460
on-prem, which talk to
Google Cloud applications

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:08.710
through the public internet.

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:10.710
And on the right-hand
side, you see the browser,

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:13.970
which is the customer
traffic, which is, again,

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:20.740
obviously coming through the
internet into the Google origin

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:22.070
URL.

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:24.290
So what we have is--

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:27.400
if you guys attended the last
session from Luke and William,

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:29.630
so this is actually
a double-click

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:32.960
on that whole
episode or session.

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:38.050
And [INAUDIBLE] spoke about
an HTTP load balancer.

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:40.340
So we use HTTP load balancers.

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:44.570
So all our requests go to
a central global HTTP LB.

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:49.300
And they get load balanced
across multiple zones

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:50.660
in multiple regions.

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:54.080
So we run on two different
regions-- eastern/central

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:57.350
and two zones in each region.

00:14:57.350 --> 00:15:02.360
And so one thing which you
can see from this diagram

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:06.480
or picture is that you
don't see any cross arrows.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:08.710
You don't see any arrows
which is going from East 1B

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:11.550
to East 1C or from
East 1 to Central.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:15.680
So basically, that's our pattern
for zone isolation, where

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:20.470
we restrict traffic to a
particular specific zone

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:21.330
for two purposes.

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:25.220
One is it reduces latency
and improves performance,

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:29.627
because your traffic
is within the zone.

00:15:29.627 --> 00:15:31.460
And it also reduces
cost, because you're not

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:35.390
paying egress cost for going
to a different zone or region.

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:41.440
More importantly, it helps us
route traffic for failures.

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:44.720
So if a specific service
is not functioning

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:49.610
or if it's down on a zone, then
the LB thinks that it's down.

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:52.200
The health check on that
LB for that service fails,

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:55.610
and the service is routed
to a different zone.

00:15:55.610 --> 00:15:58.130
Or if there is a
region failure, it

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:01.730
routes to a different region.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:07.700
So the only cross-zone
or cross-region traffic,

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:11.300
as you can see from the
picture, is the database.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:14.720
So we use Cassandra
for SR data store.

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:17.895
And that data has to be
replicated across different--

00:16:17.895 --> 00:16:20.800
those regions with all the
apps have the same state they

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:24.030
are talking to, right?

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:29.422
But once the LB
determines which zone

00:16:29.422 --> 00:16:31.130
is going to [INAUDIBLE]
the traffic based

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:34.367
on the geolocation,
it stays in that zone

00:16:34.367 --> 00:16:35.950
unless there is a
failure, in which it

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:36.991
goes to a different zone.

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:42.070
So that's the
basic architecture.

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:45.300
So we use NGINX web
tier, so that's our--

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:49.980
all our records go through a
centralized web tier or web

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:50.800
layer.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:54.260
And we have microservices
for each service.

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:58.550
So the requests go from NGINX to
the microservice Tomcat layer,

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:00.140
and from there to the DB.

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:02.510
There are other
components, but this

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:04.400
is the basic
architecture which we

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:08.609
have for all our applications.

00:17:08.609 --> 00:17:10.730
So most of the things
that I touched on here--

00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:14.000
OK, yeah, so the health
check is then available,

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.069
which will route to a
different zone or region,

00:17:16.069 --> 00:17:18.261
in case of failure.

00:17:18.261 --> 00:17:19.760
We have no cross-zone
communication,

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:23.859
except for database replication.

00:17:23.859 --> 00:17:27.960
So NGINX is the back-end service
for the global load balancer.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:30.794
So that way, if a zone
goes down or even,

00:17:30.794 --> 00:17:32.710
for that matter, if you
want to test something

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:36.670
on a specific zone, we can
just shut down that NGINX.

00:17:36.670 --> 00:17:39.590
And LB will automatically
detect that the--

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:42.090
the LB will automatically detect
that the health check fails

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:44.496
on that zone, and then
it draws the traffic

00:17:44.496 --> 00:17:45.370
to the other regions.

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:52.775
Yeah, so the fourth one
is kind of interesting--

00:17:52.775 --> 00:17:54.150
adjusting the
load-balancing mode

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:56.920
to send traffic
to all the zones.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:18:03.050
Well, so with our
pattern of on-prem--

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:06.960
it sends on-prem to the
Google Cloud, right--

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.940
our on-prem infrastructure
is hosted in central region.

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:14.340
So the request which
comes from on-prem

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:18.014
hits only the central zones.

00:18:18.014 --> 00:18:19.680
Now, what happens in
that case is that--

00:18:23.448 --> 00:18:25.110
the infrastructure
which is [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:28.680
on the other region, the east
region, is underutilized.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:34.050
And when we get some
amount of high traffic,

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:35.700
we auto-scale on
the central zones.

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:37.783
But the east ones, they
never get utilized, right?

00:18:37.783 --> 00:18:41.580
So this is one of the things
which we did recently,

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:44.310
is that we adjusted the
load-balancing mode, which

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:46.560
allowed us to send
traffic to all the zones,

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:50.730
even if the request is coming
from a specific region.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:58.800
So that allowed us to be
more effective in our cost

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:04.950
and not auto-scale unless
it's absolutely necessary.

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:07.120
Yeah, and for
internal traffic, we

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:08.950
use a service discovery tool.

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:15.330
It's a [INAUDIBLE] console,
which is, again, zone-isolated.

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:18.870
So when we have a
service-to-service call

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:23.460
in a specific zone, the
service discovery tool

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:28.110
allows us to only get the IPs
for that zone, which does not

00:19:28.110 --> 00:19:34.010
allow us to do a cross-zone or
a cross-regional call, yeah.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:36.190
That's pretty much it.

00:19:36.190 --> 00:19:38.100
LUKE STONE: So
what are the steps

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:41.202
that you take when you're
setting up a new microservice?

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:47.690
KUMAR: So are you asking about
what do we use to deploy, or--

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:49.300
LUKE STONE: Yeah,
what components

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:54.920
do you need to change
to add a microservice?

00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:58.640
KUMAR: So when we
add a microservice,

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:01.700
the only thing which we do
is we deploy a microservice.

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:04.010
So we have a CI/CD
[? pipeline- ?] model,

00:20:04.010 --> 00:20:05.960
which deploys the microservice.

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:09.080
And then we add the
context to NGINX.

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:12.150
And if there is any associated
back-end or data deal,

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:13.359
then we deploy that.

00:20:13.359 --> 00:20:14.150
So we go backwards.

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:16.220
We first deploy the
back-end component,

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:18.530
then we deploy the
microservice, and then

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:20.573
update NGINX to send
traffic to it, yeah.

00:20:20.573 --> 00:20:21.560
LUKE STONE: Nice.

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:27.340
And how do you roll out a new
version of a microservice?

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:32.130
KUMAR: Yeah, so we
use managed instance

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:34.110
groups for all our deployments.

00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:37.770
So if you want to roll
out a new deployment,

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:40.440
well, basically
a new version, we

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:45.270
deploy a new make, a new
managed instance group,

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:49.410
which basically has
the same service name.

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:53.790
And then the traffic
is sent to both the old

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:55.890
and the new instances
for a period of time,

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:58.200
before we shut down the
old instance groups.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:04.130
And then we have traffic
only on the new version.

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:05.610
LUKE STONE: Nice.

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:07.624
So are you pretty
happy with this setup?

00:21:07.624 --> 00:21:09.540
Is there anything major
that you would change?

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:16.020
KUMAR: Yeah, well, it
works for us, for now.

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:18.360
But there are some things,
actually, we are looking at.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:19.480
One of the things--

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:23.220
it works for status
applications where

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:25.630
all instances are similar.

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:27.570
But there are some
scenarios where

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:29.434
we don't want to send
traffic right away,

00:21:29.434 --> 00:21:30.600
after the instance comes up.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:31.766
We want to do some kind of--

00:21:34.147 --> 00:21:34.980
we do health checks.

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:36.563
But more than health
checks, you might

00:21:36.563 --> 00:21:39.420
have to do some validation or--

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.540
specifically for
database tiers, right, we

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:46.260
might want to do some
autowarming or some caching,

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:51.300
and make sure that there's
one cache before we send data

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:52.980
to it, right?

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:55.920
So for that, we are working
on a different model

00:21:55.920 --> 00:22:00.120
where we deploy the
instance, do health checks,

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:02.055
and then send
traffic to it, which

00:22:02.055 --> 00:22:04.890
is a different pattern than what
we are using right now, yeah.

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:08.460
JAY: So more robust
health checks,

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:11.840
more than is really capable
with HTTP load balancer

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:12.900
or even Console.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:18.090
I mean, a significant
validation of the service,

00:22:18.090 --> 00:22:21.420
that the data is
valid that we just fed

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:22.690
into it, that kind of thing.

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:26.940
LUKE STONE: Makes sense.

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:31.710
CUONG: So actually, when we
deploy, it deploy to each zone.

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:36.020
So an engineer
deploys to one zone.

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:39.420
And then if it's good, it's
brought to the next zone.

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:41.400
And if it's bad, then
we just roll it out.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:43.740
So we can do each
zone at a time,

00:22:43.740 --> 00:22:45.324
so we don't have to
do it at one shot.

00:22:45.324 --> 00:22:47.781
LUKE STONE: So do you have your
dev teams do the roll-outs,

00:22:47.781 --> 00:22:49.950
or is the SRE team always
doing the roll-outs?

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:53.370
CUONG: For production
today, it's mostly SRE.

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:56.550
But for a long lifecycle,
it's automatic.

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:59.200
We have a CI/CD that
automatically deploy it

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:04.250
once the code are committed
in a long lifecycle.

00:23:04.250 --> 00:23:07.500
But in the future, I think
we want, potentially,

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:11.405
that development can even do the
deployment themselves as well.

00:23:11.405 --> 00:23:12.780
LUKE STONE: Nice,
that's awesome.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:17.040
That's one of the SRE
principles that we

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:20.610
like to remind
people of, is always

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:24.580
have the dev team do some
portion of the production work,

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:26.490
so they know about it.

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:29.070
They have their
hands on production.

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:31.350
They're aware of
it, and then they

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:33.630
can sympathize a little bit.

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:42.100
So, Jay, what's your
experience with setting up

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:47.630
SRE in Home Depot
and working with CRE?

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:52.630
JAY: The CRE engagement
that we had was really,

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:53.550
really eye opening.

00:23:53.550 --> 00:23:55.800
We did a lot of--

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:58.350
there's a lot of
literature about SRE--

00:23:58.350 --> 00:24:04.700
probably, most notably, the
book that Google SREs wrote.

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:09.000
But sometimes it's hard
to take those practices

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:11.220
and really, really
apply them and then,

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:15.120
even when you do successfully
apply them, really making sure

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:17.600
that it's achieving
what you expected it to,

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:19.110
that you haven't left gaps.

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:26.070
And not just with that, but
with our entire architecture--

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:28.260
we've spent a little
bit of time doing this.

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:30.300
But there's obviously
a lot of things

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:32.200
that you can think
you got right.

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:37.710
But when you actually encounter
an unexpected failure mode

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:41.160
or there's a lot of things
that you can find that, hey,

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:45.330
we thought we had it
right, but we didn't.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:50.950
And the CRE engagement was
extremely valuable for us,

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:52.530
I think.

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:53.650
Just to kind of recap--

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:56.770
I know, probably, some of you
heard William and Luke's talk

00:24:56.770 --> 00:24:57.270
earlier.

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:04.981
But Google CRE came and worked
with our teams for at least--

00:25:04.981 --> 00:25:06.980
was it two or three months,
something like that?

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:09.386
LUKE STONE: It was
August until Black Friday

00:25:09.386 --> 00:25:10.560
at the end of November.

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.320
JAY: Yeah, it was an
extended engagement.

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:21.330
And basically, CRE sat down
over multiple meetings,

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:27.154
really poring over multiple
aspects of our architecture,

00:25:27.154 --> 00:25:29.070
how we did deployments,
how we were monitoring

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:32.190
things, how we did
service discovery, how

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:34.230
we were load balancing--

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:36.830
really, really dug deep.

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:41.700
I was really, actually,
shocked with how much

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:45.270
they were interested in not just
how we were using Google Cloud

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:47.570
components, but actually how--

00:25:47.570 --> 00:25:49.350
the open-source
software we were using.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:51.570
And were we using
it effectively,

00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:53.140
and what risks did
those introduce,

00:25:53.140 --> 00:25:56.220
and did we have proper
mitigations for those?

00:25:56.220 --> 00:26:01.500
Really dug deep into all aspects
of our platform architecture

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:06.470
and provided extremely
valuable feedback.

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:08.220
It was a little bit
terrifying, I think.

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:09.880
Just part of the
team of guys that

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:11.580
made a lot of the
decisions about

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:17.544
how we were going to do this
stuff, and kind of exposing all

00:26:17.544 --> 00:26:18.960
of that, and really
having to talk

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:21.416
about it with experts that--

00:26:21.416 --> 00:26:27.150
you kind of feel, maybe,
at a different class

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:31.140
and used to a different scale.

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:35.780
But it was extremely,
extremely valuable.

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:39.690
They really showed us
some things that were--

00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:43.110
some problems that we hadn't
anticipated, hadn't thought

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:45.900
about, but really, more
than that, really showing us

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:50.400
how SREs should engage with
providing that model for how

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:53.610
our SRE team should be engaging
with our development teams,

00:26:53.610 --> 00:26:55.740
with our app teams that
are building these things,

00:26:55.740 --> 00:26:59.640
and what kind of reviews we
should be doing with them,

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:03.770
I thought was probably the
most valuable out of it.

00:27:03.770 --> 00:27:06.870
The technical
issues that we found

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:09.300
and the gaps that we had--

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:11.830
obviously, that helped a lot.

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:15.810
But really showing us
how to do SRE, I think,

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:16.950
was the biggest thing.

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:19.950
LUKE STONE: Yeah,
that whole deep dive

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:25.650
was led by our tech lead Matt
Brown, who's right over there.

00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:30.040
And we had to learn a
whole bunch of stuff

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:33.707
too, because we don't
run Cassandra at Google.

00:27:33.707 --> 00:27:34.790
JAY: Right, Console, yeah.

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:38.610
LUKE STONE: So we had
to kind of figure out

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:43.500
how to analyze these things
from first principles.

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:45.180
And Matt came up
with a good rule

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:47.850
of thumb, which is
that we would look

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:54.180
at the API between your
system and Cloud API, right?

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:56.790
Just like the API is--
well, sometimes it's

00:27:56.790 --> 00:27:57.780
actually an API call.

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:00.870
But sometimes the API
is basically POSIX

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:02.970
if you're running
on a VM or whatever.

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:07.530
And then Matt's rule of thumb
is then, look one layer deeper

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:09.780
in levels of abstraction.

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:12.300
So we kind of
understand, oh, yeah.

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:13.920
Cassandra says that
it runs on a VM,

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:15.670
but actually what's it doing?

00:28:15.670 --> 00:28:18.250
What's its purpose?

00:28:18.250 --> 00:28:21.375
How much data is it going
to push, over what lengths?

00:28:21.375 --> 00:28:23.750
Is it going to be quiet, and
then all of a sudden bursty?

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:27.060
Or what's going
to happen with it?

00:28:27.060 --> 00:28:31.620
JAY: Right, yeah, like I
said, it was really great.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:35.730
And the depth with which
you guys went into,

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:39.930
not just the Google things, not
just how we were doing instance

00:28:39.930 --> 00:28:42.190
groups or how we were
using the load balancer,

00:28:42.190 --> 00:28:44.100
but really that extra
step that I think

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:45.880
provided the biggest value.

00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:47.530
I think William
mentioned it earlier.

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:49.321
I've been involved,
William's been involved

00:28:49.321 --> 00:28:52.800
with a number of
consulting engagements

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:57.715
and with other vendors,
and this was by far the--

00:28:57.715 --> 00:29:02.080
at least in my opinion,
was the most useful one.

00:29:02.080 --> 00:29:03.580
LUKE STONE: Yeah,
before we started,

00:29:03.580 --> 00:29:08.480
we were kind of trying to
tell William what to expect.

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:10.537
And we said, you know,
William, this is not

00:29:10.537 --> 00:29:11.620
the white-glove treatment.

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:14.280
This is more like the
rubber-glove treatment.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:15.314
JAY: Yeah, it was.

00:29:15.314 --> 00:29:16.480
LUKE STONE: And it was cool.

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:18.396
JAY: And that's why I
said it was a little bit

00:29:18.396 --> 00:29:19.870
terrifying at first, right?

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:25.810
Man, we're really going to
put all our cards on the table

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:29.710
here and have people
really pick this apart.

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:32.350
But it's better--
obviously, it's

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:38.080
better in advance than doing
this with your CIO or something

00:29:38.080 --> 00:29:41.260
after a major, major
incident, right?

00:29:41.260 --> 00:29:43.390
LUKE STONE: Yeah,
that's kind of our--

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:47.560
that's kind of the idea is that,
well, you have postmortems,

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:50.050
and you will continually
improve if you use postmortems

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:50.680
correctly.

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:53.320
You might as well start
off at a pretty good level

00:29:53.320 --> 00:29:53.940
to begin with.

00:29:53.940 --> 00:29:55.270
JAY: Right, absolutely.

00:29:55.270 --> 00:29:58.900
LUKE STONE: So how has it
affected your engineering

00:29:58.900 --> 00:30:01.270
culture to have an SRE
team, because you're pretty

00:30:01.270 --> 00:30:04.630
new within your organization?

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:08.020
JAY: It's obviously-- I think
we kind of alluded to it.

00:30:08.020 --> 00:30:10.210
It's still a little
bit incremental.

00:30:10.210 --> 00:30:16.410
We're still trying to get better
and to do things a little bit

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:17.410
better every day, right?

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:22.180
And I think that that's
the biggest thing is just--

00:30:22.180 --> 00:30:24.045
we've kind of had--

00:30:24.045 --> 00:30:26.390
it's the way, at least
in my experience--

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:29.530
I've been with Home
Depot for a while.

00:30:29.530 --> 00:30:32.200
And really being
able to establish

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:35.890
SLAs and say, what
error rate is OK, right?

00:30:35.890 --> 00:30:39.970
Is this a problem?

00:30:39.970 --> 00:30:41.180
Historically, we've kind of--

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:43.180
well, it could be better.

00:30:43.180 --> 00:30:45.700
So let's keep it
better, and really

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:47.539
having that establishment
of, well, OK.

00:30:47.539 --> 00:30:49.330
What's really the number
that we care about

00:30:49.330 --> 00:30:53.250
and the numbers that we need
to achieve for our business?

00:30:53.250 --> 00:30:56.867
And then, as long as
we're under that, then

00:30:56.867 --> 00:30:58.450
we don't have to
worry as much, right?

00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:03.930
That's a big change
for us, I think.

00:31:03.930 --> 00:31:06.250
I think that's really
adjusted our mentality,

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:13.000
and it's kind of helped to
make the operations SRE role

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:16.420
a little bit more sustainable,
which is important,

00:31:16.420 --> 00:31:17.995
because we've--

00:31:17.995 --> 00:31:21.130
I've been doing this for a
while, and a lot of times,

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:24.790
it's been extremely,
extremely taxing, right?

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:26.800
I'm sure we could share
war stories for a while.

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:30.640
But it's been a
lot of always on,

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:33.310
a lot of burning the
candle at both ends,

00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:37.300
trying to get things done, keep
things up, get things ready.

00:31:37.300 --> 00:31:42.795
And for anybody, it
takes a toll, right?

00:31:42.795 --> 00:31:44.170
You can only do
that for so long.

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:47.470
LUKE STONE: Yeah, have you
noticed any significant changes

00:31:47.470 --> 00:31:49.336
in your development velocity?

00:31:53.410 --> 00:31:56.500
JAY: Probably-- I haven't
really tracked it that closely.

00:31:56.500 --> 00:31:58.060
I think that we probably have.

00:31:58.060 --> 00:32:02.710
I think that especially
for our application team,

00:32:02.710 --> 00:32:07.400
we've provided kind
of the paved road of,

00:32:07.400 --> 00:32:09.017
hey, here's a
pattern that works.

00:32:09.017 --> 00:32:10.100
Here's a model that works.

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:13.787
And if you follow
that path, things

00:32:13.787 --> 00:32:15.370
will generally be
pretty good for you,

00:32:15.370 --> 00:32:17.650
and you won't have
a lot of trouble.

00:32:17.650 --> 00:32:21.220
And certainly, once
an app is launched,

00:32:21.220 --> 00:32:23.390
I think that we have--

00:32:23.390 --> 00:32:24.910
and it's not just SRE.

00:32:24.910 --> 00:32:31.060
I think that it's a number of
principles of solid testing.

00:32:31.060 --> 00:32:36.880
And just our overall
software engineering practice

00:32:36.880 --> 00:32:38.590
is also kind of changing
at the same time

00:32:38.590 --> 00:32:40.050
that we're implementing SRE.

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:44.080
And that's definitely
provided us

00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:47.530
a lift in how quick we
can get things rolled out.

00:32:47.530 --> 00:32:53.574
LUKE STONE: Yeah, I notice you
guys centralized your CI/CD.

00:32:53.574 --> 00:32:54.990
And you have an
engineer, Christo,

00:32:54.990 --> 00:33:00.370
who's making a really
great CI/CD platform

00:33:00.370 --> 00:33:02.530
that all the dev teams can use.

00:33:02.530 --> 00:33:05.470
And he got out ahead
of the problem,

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:08.280
and so now everybody just
uses it, and it's solved.

00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:11.230
JAY: Yeah, and as
long as you use that,

00:33:11.230 --> 00:33:16.600
and you follow a few not
overly constraining practices,

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:19.000
if you follow that,
then it's pretty

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:21.949
easy to get your app
running and to deploy

00:33:21.949 --> 00:33:22.990
new versions of your app.

00:33:22.990 --> 00:33:25.870
And it just kind
of works, right.

00:33:25.870 --> 00:33:28.120
LUKE STONE: So one thing
William was mentioning

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:31.240
was how your strategy is--

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:34.920
you have this monolithic
legacy architecture

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:37.840
that's sitting on-prem in
your data center, right?

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:40.750
And your strategy
has been to bite off

00:33:40.750 --> 00:33:45.200
one microservice at a time and
implement that microservice

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:46.410
in the cloud, right?

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:49.410
And then you just imagine,
like you take a class

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:51.570
and move it to the cloud,
and it's in the server.

00:33:51.570 --> 00:33:54.460
And then you just call
to it over an RPC.

00:33:54.460 --> 00:33:56.620
How does that work for you?

00:33:56.620 --> 00:33:58.740
JAY: It's been a
journey that we've

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:03.630
been on for a while, of breaking
components out of our monolith

00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:08.205
and deploying those
separately, even

00:34:08.205 --> 00:34:09.330
in our on-prem environment.

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:12.389
We've been on this journey
for a little while.

00:34:12.389 --> 00:34:16.690
Definitely, moving it
out of your data center

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:20.909
adds some additional
challenges, right?

00:34:20.909 --> 00:34:23.880
What was maybe a few
milliseconds of latency

00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:26.310
now becomes--

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:30.239
I think we've got roughly, what,
40, 50 milliseconds of latency

00:34:30.239 --> 00:34:32.760
to our services in Google.

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:36.221
So that's been a little
bit of a challenge of,

00:34:36.221 --> 00:34:37.179
how do you manage that?

00:34:37.179 --> 00:34:39.120
And so you have
to carefully plan

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:41.269
how you're going to
move components and move

00:34:41.269 --> 00:34:42.060
their dependencies.

00:34:42.060 --> 00:34:46.855
And do you move a
back-end API to Google

00:34:46.855 --> 00:34:48.480
before you move the
front end, and then

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:50.820
have your customers
kind of ping-ponging

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:54.360
between the components?

00:34:54.360 --> 00:34:56.969
Sometimes the unfortunate
answer is, sometimes

00:34:56.969 --> 00:34:58.290
we have to do that, right?

00:34:58.290 --> 00:35:00.880
We've got to be able to get
the service out, running.

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:02.520
We've got to make sure that
it's actually functional.

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:03.936
Well, then the
only way to do that

00:35:03.936 --> 00:35:06.860
is to put customer
traffic on it.

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:10.600
And if we have to eat a little
bit of latency in there,

00:35:10.600 --> 00:35:15.089
then we make that as a kind of
an informed decision and say,

00:35:15.089 --> 00:35:17.005
hey, this is going to
be the state we're going

00:35:17.005 --> 00:35:18.171
to be in for a little while.

00:35:18.171 --> 00:35:21.690
And other times it's, well, we
don't really have to do that.

00:35:21.690 --> 00:35:23.550
We can move the
front end as well,

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:28.910
and maybe move both
components at the same time.

00:35:28.910 --> 00:35:31.080
So it's kind of a
case-by-case thing

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:33.380
of how we're going to do that.

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:35.820
But yeah, in general,
it's worked pretty well.

00:35:38.670 --> 00:35:40.460
I think it's a good model.

00:35:40.460 --> 00:35:42.180
LUKE STONE: How
do you decide how

00:35:42.180 --> 00:35:45.260
big to make the microservice?

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:48.114
JAY: That's a great question.

00:35:48.114 --> 00:35:49.530
Definitely, I think
we've got some

00:35:49.530 --> 00:35:53.104
that we feel like
maybe are maybe

00:35:53.104 --> 00:35:54.270
smaller than they should be.

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:58.050
And I think we've
definitely got some that are

00:35:58.050 --> 00:35:59.640
larger than we would like them.

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:02.430
But that's an evolution, right?

00:36:02.430 --> 00:36:06.120
I think, especially
once we migrate and--

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:08.610
it becomes relatively easy.

00:36:08.610 --> 00:36:13.110
On-prem, we had--
creating a new cluster,

00:36:13.110 --> 00:36:16.050
deploying a new application,
there was a lot of process

00:36:16.050 --> 00:36:19.810
and a lot of things you had
to go through to do that.

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:23.280
It just didn't-- there's not an
API you can call and get a new

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:24.600
set of servers racked, right?

00:36:24.600 --> 00:36:26.880
You had to-- sometimes
it was procurement.

00:36:26.880 --> 00:36:28.677
Sometimes it was
multiple teams that you

00:36:28.677 --> 00:36:30.510
would have to go through
to get them cabled,

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:33.270
and get firewall policies,
and all that stuff.

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:38.722
So I think at least my
opinion is that once

00:36:38.722 --> 00:36:40.680
you've migrated to the
app, even if it's bigger

00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:44.260
than you wanted it to be
and wasn't perfectly sized

00:36:44.260 --> 00:36:50.130
microservice, textbook, whatever
that is, that you can always

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:54.770
come back and break it down
later, and divide it up

00:36:54.770 --> 00:36:55.740
as it makes sense.

00:36:55.740 --> 00:36:57.630
And it's much easier
then, because now you've

00:36:57.630 --> 00:37:03.210
got a system that's basically
designed to change and adapt

00:37:03.210 --> 00:37:04.810
much more fluidly.

00:37:04.810 --> 00:37:10.620
LUKE STONE: Yeah, so if you've
got 70, 80 microservices,

00:37:10.620 --> 00:37:14.714
you're going to have a lot of
logging and monitoring streams.

00:37:14.714 --> 00:37:16.380
How do you keep this
stuff all straight?

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:18.960
JAY: Well, I don't know
that we've hit it out

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:20.860
of the park on all of it.

00:37:20.860 --> 00:37:26.310
We're definitely using
Stackdriver for our core

00:37:26.310 --> 00:37:29.770
metrics, and we've had to get
creative with some things,

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:30.270
right?

00:37:35.230 --> 00:37:39.510
I'm also using Stackdriver,
Google logging, and Fluentd

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:44.340
Agent to push all of our
log streams into logging API

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:47.170
and ultimately into BigQuery.

00:37:47.170 --> 00:37:48.759
We've had a few
challenges with having

00:37:48.759 --> 00:37:51.300
to get our data structured well
enough that we could actually

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:54.540
utilize it effectively and be
able to run queries against it.

00:37:54.540 --> 00:37:56.860
And then we've built tools,
like we were mentioning

00:37:56.860 --> 00:37:59.780
with the VALET dashboard
that we had there that

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:02.430
shows the aggregate metrics.

00:38:02.430 --> 00:38:06.630
We've built tools to make it
easier for big queries to get

00:38:06.630 --> 00:38:09.930
run-- basically, an API that
you can call to run the query

00:38:09.930 --> 00:38:12.540
for gathering an hour,
or two hours, or a day,

00:38:12.540 --> 00:38:16.170
or whatever time frame you
need of the metrics for that

00:38:16.170 --> 00:38:23.100
application, and then being able
to expose it in multiple ways--

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:29.010
a chat bot or being
able to push it

00:38:29.010 --> 00:38:34.680
into a long-term kind
of aggregated metrics,

00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:36.510
like the VALET dashboard.

00:38:36.510 --> 00:38:40.600
And then we've done some things
even with the Stackdriver

00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:41.550
collectd that--

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:48.430
we're still working towards
actually having our application

00:38:48.430 --> 00:38:49.870
submit the metrics.

00:38:49.870 --> 00:38:52.750
So we got creative with some
of the things that were capable

00:38:52.750 --> 00:38:55.420
there and did use
[INAUDIBLE] plugin

00:38:55.420 --> 00:39:00.100
to scrape our real-time
metrics that we wanted out

00:39:00.100 --> 00:39:03.150
of the log files as well.

00:39:03.150 --> 00:39:05.830
Obviously, we're working on
trying to improve that and make

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:06.920
it more scalable.

00:39:06.920 --> 00:39:10.150
It's got a little-- there's
a few problems with it.

00:39:10.150 --> 00:39:13.310
It's a little more hands-on
than we want it to be.

00:39:13.310 --> 00:39:17.020
So we're trying to come up
with new and creative ways

00:39:17.020 --> 00:39:23.276
to gather that without having to
have such a dependence on logs.

00:39:23.276 --> 00:39:25.020
LUKE STONE: Yeah, I liked--

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:28.780
a couple things you did
seemed pretty smart.

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:30.790
There were some
Slack bots that you

00:39:30.790 --> 00:39:34.042
built that would put a bunch
of stuff into a Slack channel.

00:39:34.042 --> 00:39:36.250
So people could just kind
of watch the Slack channel,

00:39:36.250 --> 00:39:38.030
and you'd have a pretty good
idea of what's going on.

00:39:38.030 --> 00:39:39.100
That was pretty cool.

00:39:39.100 --> 00:39:42.140
JAY: Yeah, and we've used
that fairly effectively.

00:39:42.140 --> 00:39:44.410
And it's basically the same--

00:39:44.410 --> 00:39:46.447
the site bot's relatively dumb.

00:39:46.447 --> 00:39:47.530
It just presents the data.

00:39:47.530 --> 00:39:55.570
But the actual application that
we wrote that we call our TPS

00:39:55.570 --> 00:39:56.920
reports, which is--

00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:59.710
basically, it just
goes out, and you

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:01.810
can feed it an application
name and a time frame

00:40:01.810 --> 00:40:06.220
and it will go query
BigQuery for that

00:40:06.220 --> 00:40:09.040
and provide back the data.

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:11.780
And I think even, in
some cases, it actually

00:40:11.780 --> 00:40:15.130
notes that compared
to the SLO of what

00:40:15.130 --> 00:40:16.720
the application's expecting.

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:20.500
So you can clearly see if,
even on a one-hour time frame

00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:23.560
or a custom time frame,
whatever you want--

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:25.300
is this app healthy?

00:40:25.300 --> 00:40:26.035
Is it OK?

00:40:26.035 --> 00:40:28.390
And anybody can query that.

00:40:28.390 --> 00:40:31.810
It's available to the
whole company, right?

00:40:31.810 --> 00:40:35.686
Anybody can join our Slack
and run those queries.

00:40:35.686 --> 00:40:37.060
LUKE STONE: Yeah,
the other thing

00:40:37.060 --> 00:40:39.324
I thought was pretty
cool that came up

00:40:39.324 --> 00:40:40.990
when we were doing a
wheel of misfortune

00:40:40.990 --> 00:40:45.130
one time was you had your
deployment script log

00:40:45.130 --> 00:40:48.010
everything that it
was doing to BigQuery.

00:40:48.010 --> 00:40:50.499
So you could go into
BigQuery and be like, OK,

00:40:50.499 --> 00:40:51.790
when did this deployment start?

00:40:51.790 --> 00:40:52.625
Who did it?

00:40:52.625 --> 00:40:53.500
What's going on here?

00:40:53.500 --> 00:40:54.390
That was pretty cool.

00:40:54.390 --> 00:40:57.290
JAY: Yeah, that's a
more direct integration.

00:40:57.290 --> 00:41:02.110
So we use Fluentd on our
nodes to collect the logs--

00:41:02.110 --> 00:41:03.930
not just our application
logs, but other--

00:41:03.930 --> 00:41:09.900
Syslog and other general
logs of interest.

00:41:09.900 --> 00:41:13.150
But our deployment tool actually
logs directly to the API,

00:41:13.150 --> 00:41:16.810
and it actually is
very clear about what--

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:19.300
you can see exactly what the
deployment tool is doing,

00:41:19.300 --> 00:41:22.060
even if you're not sitting
in front of the screen,

00:41:22.060 --> 00:41:24.700
running the deployments
yourself, right?

00:41:24.700 --> 00:41:28.110
It's fairly real-time to
see what's going on there.

00:41:28.110 --> 00:41:29.110
LUKE STONE: That's cool.

00:41:29.110 --> 00:41:32.770
So what technologies are
you looking forward to?

00:41:32.770 --> 00:41:36.480
What are you going to adopt
as soon as it becomes GA?

00:41:38.980 --> 00:41:39.730
JAY: I don't know.

00:41:39.730 --> 00:41:47.320
We kind of worked around the
lack of internal load balancers

00:41:47.320 --> 00:41:50.812
early on, and so that's
one thing that we've

00:41:50.812 --> 00:41:51.520
been considering.

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:53.180
Should we revisit that?

00:41:53.180 --> 00:41:58.590
Should we look back at--

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:00.910
since the internal load
balancers are GA now,

00:42:00.910 --> 00:42:03.190
should we evaluate
using that instead

00:42:03.190 --> 00:42:09.500
of our home-grown
solution that we use?

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:10.750
Other than that, I don't know.

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:15.730
Spanner looks really cool, but I
could probably geek out all day

00:42:15.730 --> 00:42:16.530
and say--

00:42:16.530 --> 00:42:19.330
and as a matter of fact,
through our engagement,

00:42:19.330 --> 00:42:25.300
we've had so many Google
product teams wanting--

00:42:25.300 --> 00:42:27.820
like, hey, we've got
this thing that's coming.

00:42:27.820 --> 00:42:30.070
Would you be
interested in doing--

00:42:30.070 --> 00:42:31.660
testing with it, alpha testing?

00:42:31.660 --> 00:42:32.795
We'd like your feedback.

00:42:32.795 --> 00:42:36.659
And it was kind of like,
you could definitely get

00:42:36.659 --> 00:42:38.200
overwhelmed with
the all-you-can-eat,

00:42:38.200 --> 00:42:40.210
because then once they ask you
to do that, and you say, oh,

00:42:40.210 --> 00:42:40.390
yeah.

00:42:40.390 --> 00:42:41.098
That sounds cool.

00:42:41.098 --> 00:42:43.690
And then they want feedback
on, how did it work,

00:42:43.690 --> 00:42:46.060
and what did you
think, and all that.

00:42:46.060 --> 00:42:49.450
So it's kind of a--
you can definitely

00:42:49.450 --> 00:42:51.676
overload on that, so.

00:42:51.676 --> 00:42:53.170
KUMAR: XBN.

00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:57.800
JAY: Yeah, so XBN is something
that we've been interested in.

00:42:57.800 --> 00:43:01.450
So right now, we've kind
of got some growing pains.

00:43:01.450 --> 00:43:03.940
Within our project, we're
kind of consolidated

00:43:03.940 --> 00:43:09.460
with most of our applications,
all in one project.

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:14.920
And we've run into some problems
there with API quota limits.

00:43:14.920 --> 00:43:18.320
And some of those are soft
limits that we can get raised,

00:43:18.320 --> 00:43:21.010
and some of them are
like, no, we really

00:43:21.010 --> 00:43:24.520
can't change that for you.

00:43:24.520 --> 00:43:27.430
So we're considering
XBN and using

00:43:27.430 --> 00:43:30.040
XBN to allow us to
spread our services out

00:43:30.040 --> 00:43:32.980
over multiple projects to
still communicate internally

00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:35.680
and fairly openly and
freely across that,

00:43:35.680 --> 00:43:40.482
but to be able to decompose
down to multiple projects.

00:43:40.482 --> 00:43:41.190
LUKE STONE: Nice.

00:43:43.840 --> 00:43:45.924
So I was wondering if we
had any questions-- yeah?

00:43:45.924 --> 00:43:47.381
AUDIENCE: Are you
taking questions?

00:43:47.381 --> 00:43:48.750
LUKE STONE: Yes, please do.

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:49.958
I think this is a great time.

00:43:49.958 --> 00:43:51.180
We got about 12 minutes left.

00:43:53.880 --> 00:43:58.440
AUDIENCE: So during the keynote
this morning, your Home Depot

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:00.760
guy mentioned that you
have moved part of the data

00:44:00.760 --> 00:44:03.230
warehouse into
BigQuery, and then he

00:44:03.230 --> 00:44:07.690
avoided an answer about the
problems that you were having.

00:44:07.690 --> 00:44:10.570
JAY: And if our SVP avoided
it, I probably will to.

00:44:14.350 --> 00:44:16.930
We use BigQuery,
in our operation,

00:44:16.930 --> 00:44:19.800
mainly for log analysis.

00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:21.840
And we've got a few other
use cases we're using.

00:44:21.840 --> 00:44:24.700
We have another team that's
doing data warehousing,

00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:27.100
and they can probably
answer your questions

00:44:27.100 --> 00:44:28.610
much better than I can.

00:44:28.610 --> 00:44:29.110
I don't--

00:44:29.110 --> 00:44:33.520
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:41.320 --> 00:44:43.320
LUKE STONE: Bring back
the mic so they can talk.

00:44:43.320 --> 00:44:46.260
AUDIENCE: Yeah, yeah.

00:44:46.260 --> 00:44:48.974
LUKE STONE: Have
you been helped?

00:44:48.974 --> 00:44:50.390
AUDIENCE: So he
avoided the answer

00:44:50.390 --> 00:44:52.320
in order to say
why not succeeded,

00:44:52.320 --> 00:44:54.367
and then he was arguing
about looking for talent

00:44:54.367 --> 00:44:56.450
and working off interesting
problems and so forth.

00:44:58.975 --> 00:45:00.100
LUKE STONE: This is Shashi.

00:45:00.100 --> 00:45:02.930
He's also an SRE at Home Depot.

00:45:02.930 --> 00:45:05.060
SHASHI: We have two teams.

00:45:05.060 --> 00:45:08.700
One of them is being our
ADW team, warehouse team.

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:10.550
They started using BigQuery.

00:45:10.550 --> 00:45:13.730
I don't recall them mentioning
any issues with that.

00:45:13.730 --> 00:45:16.270
We are the e-commerce
team, make sense?

00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:21.160
AUDIENCE: Especially when
it comes down to, let's say,

00:45:21.160 --> 00:45:23.667
unpredicted queries,
so these joint issues

00:45:23.667 --> 00:45:24.750
and these kind of things--

00:45:24.750 --> 00:45:26.980
there is not really an
issue, performance-wise?

00:45:26.980 --> 00:45:29.950
So performance is good, from
your point of view, yeah?

00:45:29.950 --> 00:45:33.400
SHASHI: So from other
[INAUDIBLE] sorry.

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:35.190
From the e-commerce
point of view,

00:45:35.190 --> 00:45:37.650
you're asking about
the select queries,

00:45:37.650 --> 00:45:40.750
whether when you are doing
some views on top of it,

00:45:40.750 --> 00:45:43.780
how is the performance of the
BigQuery and all that stuff.

00:45:43.780 --> 00:45:47.510
For our reporting, e-commerce
reporting purposes, logging

00:45:47.510 --> 00:45:50.260
reporting purposes, whatever
the queries we are running,

00:45:50.260 --> 00:45:54.534
we do not see any
issues, as of now.

00:45:54.534 --> 00:45:55.950
KUMAR: Yeah, so
the one area where

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:58.570
we use BigQuery is that
all our transactional data

00:45:58.570 --> 00:46:03.750
gets fed into BigQuery through
an incoming job or a process.

00:46:03.750 --> 00:46:06.220
And the production
operations, then they

00:46:06.220 --> 00:46:10.420
will go to our transactional
data store to query something.

00:46:10.420 --> 00:46:13.930
They go to BigQuery, and it's
working out pretty well for us.

00:46:16.680 --> 00:46:19.990
AUDIENCE: OK, so before
we had the SRE book,

00:46:19.990 --> 00:46:22.090
we had the testing
book from Google.

00:46:22.090 --> 00:46:25.240
So I'm wondering how the SREs
interact with the software

00:46:25.240 --> 00:46:27.505
engineer testing teams.

00:46:30.610 --> 00:46:32.470
LUKE STONE: Shashi again.

00:46:32.470 --> 00:46:36.940
SHASHI: So we do have a
performance engineering team.

00:46:36.940 --> 00:46:42.550
They do all the stress
tests on our microservices.

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:44.780
And now what we have,
transitioning that

00:46:44.780 --> 00:46:46.460
into SRE teams--

00:46:46.460 --> 00:46:48.250
so now SRE teams
and dev teams, we

00:46:48.250 --> 00:46:51.850
are empowering them to do
their own stress tests,

00:46:51.850 --> 00:46:55.181
so that when they
deploy their service--

00:46:55.181 --> 00:46:57.680
before deploying their service,
they can do the stress test,

00:46:57.680 --> 00:47:01.230
load test, check their SLOs,
volumetrics, and all that kind

00:47:01.230 --> 00:47:02.387
of good stuff.

00:47:02.387 --> 00:47:03.970
And then once they
get a clean signal,

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:06.140
it's part of the CI/CD flow.

00:47:06.140 --> 00:47:07.880
That's what we are
planning, and then it

00:47:07.880 --> 00:47:08.920
will get a green signal.

00:47:08.920 --> 00:47:10.620
And then they can
go to production.

00:47:15.362 --> 00:47:16.820
AUDIENCE: So you
mentioned you have

00:47:16.820 --> 00:47:21.230
a large, monolithic, on-prem
system still running.

00:47:21.230 --> 00:47:25.130
I wonder what criteria you
use to peel off functionality

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:28.580
from the monolithic system
to build these microservices.

00:47:31.240 --> 00:47:32.900
JAY: So I'll take
this opportunity

00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:36.650
to plug my boss's talk tomorrow.

00:47:36.650 --> 00:47:41.110
So he's actually going to talk
pretty extensively about this.

00:47:41.110 --> 00:47:43.090
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:43.090 --> 00:47:46.640
JAY: But I think
the short answer is,

00:47:46.640 --> 00:47:48.920
based on what's easiest, right?

00:47:48.920 --> 00:47:51.350
That's where we kind of
started with this was,

00:47:51.350 --> 00:47:55.770
what were the easier
things to separate?

00:47:55.770 --> 00:47:59.990
And additionally, what things,
what components of the site

00:47:59.990 --> 00:48:03.560
did we want to be able to
update more rapidly than what

00:48:03.560 --> 00:48:09.200
it was really feasible to do
with the monolithic components?

00:48:09.200 --> 00:48:12.980
So those were probably the
two kind of core things.

00:48:16.610 --> 00:48:20.630
AUDIENCE: How much of your
time you spend on automation

00:48:20.630 --> 00:48:22.250
versus operations?

00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:25.610
JAY: I don't know.

00:48:25.610 --> 00:48:30.350
I don't know if I have a
good, hard metric on that.

00:48:30.350 --> 00:48:35.030
I would say that, probably,
too much time on operations

00:48:35.030 --> 00:48:35.630
right now--

00:48:35.630 --> 00:48:40.100
I think we really want to
get more to automating.

00:48:40.100 --> 00:48:48.092
It's, I would say, probably
50-50 at worst case right now.

00:48:48.092 --> 00:48:49.550
I think that a lot
of our time is--

00:48:53.120 --> 00:48:54.630
KUMAR: Yeah, it
probably started off

00:48:54.630 --> 00:48:57.840
at 20-80, maybe 20
automation and 80 operations.

00:48:57.840 --> 00:49:00.330
But I think we are, right
now, close to 50-50.

00:49:00.330 --> 00:49:02.780
I think we are getting better.

00:49:02.780 --> 00:49:05.400
There is more
automated deployments

00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:08.060
and automated processes
which we have in place.

00:49:08.060 --> 00:49:11.120
So one of the things which
we still need to work on

00:49:11.120 --> 00:49:13.200
is automated tests.

00:49:13.200 --> 00:49:18.600
We are not 100% on
that, so we still

00:49:18.600 --> 00:49:21.460
have to do a lot of
manual validation.

00:49:21.460 --> 00:49:24.790
So that's something which
we're working on, yeah.

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:30.962
AUDIENCE: Hi, how do you
divide application support

00:49:30.962 --> 00:49:31.670
across the teams?

00:49:31.670 --> 00:49:34.920
So how is the
application architecture

00:49:34.920 --> 00:49:39.610
itself divided among the
teams, in terms of support?

00:49:39.610 --> 00:49:41.640
KUMAR: Architecture
or just support?

00:49:41.640 --> 00:49:43.020
AUDIENCE: The product itself--

00:49:43.020 --> 00:49:44.595
how are the
applications divided?

00:49:49.470 --> 00:49:50.220
SHASHI: I'm sorry.

00:49:50.220 --> 00:49:53.090
Can you repeat one more, just
the last sentence, last one?

00:49:53.090 --> 00:49:57.040
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so
the product is divided

00:49:57.040 --> 00:49:58.110
into multiple components.

00:49:58.110 --> 00:49:58.610
SHASHI: Yes.

00:49:58.610 --> 00:50:01.200
AUDIENCE: And SRE
teams are handling,

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:04.190
managing, maintaining, and
supporting these components.

00:50:04.190 --> 00:50:06.860
My question is,
how many components

00:50:06.860 --> 00:50:10.610
or how much of this product
is divided between teams?

00:50:10.610 --> 00:50:13.640
So how many application
does each team support?

00:50:13.640 --> 00:50:16.230
SHASHI: Oh, you mean each
SRE, how many applications

00:50:16.230 --> 00:50:16.730
it supports?

00:50:16.730 --> 00:50:19.060
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:50:19.060 --> 00:50:21.260
SHASHI: There is no
hard-and-fast rule for it.

00:50:21.260 --> 00:50:23.330
It depends on the
velocity of the changes.

00:50:23.330 --> 00:50:27.080
It depends on the critical
nature of the application,

00:50:27.080 --> 00:50:31.730
so we have primary SREs, that's
what you're talking about,

00:50:31.730 --> 00:50:33.620
so primary SREs, secondary SREs.

00:50:33.620 --> 00:50:35.730
And then the one SRE would
be on call, that kind

00:50:35.730 --> 00:50:37.160
of operational issues.

00:50:37.160 --> 00:50:39.270
But there is no
hard-and-fast rule for that--

00:50:39.270 --> 00:50:41.750
one application should
have 10 SREs or five SREs.

00:50:41.750 --> 00:50:43.200
There is no rule for that.

00:50:43.200 --> 00:50:44.730
It depends on the application.

00:50:44.730 --> 00:50:45.271
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:50:48.650 --> 00:50:52.700
JAY: We do generally
bucket our applications up

00:50:52.700 --> 00:50:55.640
between what we call
the browse components

00:50:55.640 --> 00:50:59.450
of our site versus the My
Cart and Checkout components.

00:50:59.450 --> 00:51:02.719
And so we have separate
SRE teams that--

00:51:02.719 --> 00:51:04.760
they work closely in
conjunction with each other.

00:51:04.760 --> 00:51:08.390
But applications are generally
bucketed towards that.

00:51:08.390 --> 00:51:12.800
Obviously, we've got a lot that
kind of fit the middle ground.

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:14.780
They're not perfectly
one nor the other.

00:51:14.780 --> 00:51:17.570
They're maybe
shared or whatever,

00:51:17.570 --> 00:51:20.720
but, generally, we assign
them to one team or the other.

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:22.220
And then within
each of those teams,

00:51:22.220 --> 00:51:26.630
there's multiple
SREs that divide

00:51:26.630 --> 00:51:29.420
those up with expertise
on each one, deeper

00:51:29.420 --> 00:51:31.640
knowledge on each one.

